Packaged .vsix files also ship the unmodified verible-verilog-format binary from Verible
(Apache License 2.0; Copyright The Verible Authors; https://github.com/chipsalliance/verible)
with its LICENSE file, and the npm packages listed in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt, among them
elkjs, used under the Eclipse Public License 2.0 (source: https://github.com/kieler/elkjs).
The extension also ships the SystemVerilog and Verilog TextMate grammars of the
Verilog-HDL/SystemVerilog/Bluespec SystemVerilog extension 1.29.0 (MIT License; Copyright (c)
2016-2022 Masahiro H; https://github.com/mshr-h/vscode-verilog-hdl-support) in client/syntaxes/.
That extension's SystemVerilog grammar derives from VSCode-SystemVerilog (MIT License; Copyright (c)
2018 Eirik Prestegårdshus; https://github.com/eirikpre/VSCode-SystemVerilog) and its Verilog grammar
from the TextMate verilog.tmbundle (https://github.com/textmate/verilog.tmbundle, under its
permissive licence). All three notices are in client/third_party/verilog-hdl-LICENSE.

The language server depends on pyslang (MIT), pygls (Apache License 2.0) and lsprotocol
(MIT). The generic .vsix and the PyPI package install them from PyPI. The linux-x64 .vsix
ships, unmodified from PyPI, the wheels of those packages and of their dependencies (attrs,
cattrs, typing_extensions and nanobind's backend wheel, which carries nanobind's BSD-3-Clause
licence in client/third_party/nanobind-LICENSE); every licence text is in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt.
The built-in UVM macro shim in server/uvmls/shim/ is derived from the Accellera UVM reference
implementation (Apache License 2.0) and says so in its header.

